  "summary": "Five new reports automate Patch-seq, atlas building, glomerular mapping and spatial transcript analysis, and a Drosophila study ties one isoform to connectome identity.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is scaling quantitative neuronal characterization through automation and computation. Patch-seq work shows reusable pipettes with robotic nucleus extraction preserve electrophysiology and transcriptomic yield. In C. elegans, near-wild-type probabilistic atlases improve variability-aware identification to 86.3% top-1. Whole-bulb light-sheet expansion microscopy with automated segmentation maps thousands of olfactory glomeruli in intact mouse bulbs. SCOUBI infers axon-dendrite interfaces from extrasomatic transcripts in spatial transcriptomics. A Drosophila study provides a mechanistic counterpoint: the broad-z4 isoform shifts LPLC2 toward an LPLC1-like type in morphology, connectivity and transcriptome. The items differ in organism and method, but together they couple molecular identity to structure and connectivity at higher throughput.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
